  if err != nil {
      return Order{}, fmt.Errorf("fetching order %s: %w", id, err)
  }
  ```
- Reach for `%v` only when you deliberately want to cut the chain — rare; the caller you can't see yet is who `errors.Is`/`errors.As` serves, so keep the chain open by default.
- If your package defines sentinel errors (e.g. `ErrOrderNotFound`), return them directly or wrapped, and let downstream callers test with `errors.Is`. Don't compare error strings.
